---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
---

<CaseStudy
  eyebrow="Interactive tool · Pro bono · Oct 2026"
  title="A planting planner a landowner can use on their phone"
  outcome="A one-file, phone-first map of a 102-dönüm field: tap an area, pull a corner to resize it, pick a crop, see what it costs. Sent to a non-technical landowner as a WhatsApp attachment."
  role="Research, UX, UI, build (solo)"
  timeline="2 days · 78 published versions"
  tools={["Vanilla HTML/CSS/JS", "SVG", "Python (parametric model)", "Cadastre parcel API", "Satellite imagery"]}
  liveUrl="/work/kilya/demo.html"
>
  <h2>Problem &amp; constraints</h2>
  <p>
    A family friend owns a 102-dönüm field (about 10 hectares) on the Gallipoli
    peninsula and wanted to know what could be planted on it, and roughly what each
    option would cost. The brief arrived as a hand sketch and two WhatsApp messages:
    an olive-spacing table and a yield timeline.
  </p>
  <p>
    The person who would use the result is middle-aged, not comfortable with
    technology, and would open it on a phone. Their one explicit request was that it
    be <em>"very, very simple"</em>. Everything else followed from that: no jargon,
    body text never below 17&nbsp;px, 52&nbsp;px buttons, no icon-only controls, and
    a page that works as a single HTML file attached to a chat message.
  </p>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/kilya/sketch.jpg"
    alt="Hand-drawn sketch of the field: forest at the top, a dirt track down the middle, pecan along the track, olives at the top, a house and barn on the right, sunflower and alfalfa at the bottom."
    caption="The brief. A hand sketch of the field with the owner's first idea of what goes where."
  />

  <h2>Process</h2>
  <p>
    I started from the real parcel instead of the sketch. The boundary came from the
    national cadastre service, converted to local metres and rotated so the existing
    dirt track runs straight up the page. A grid of 1-dönüm squares (the unit every
    Turkish farmer thinks in) is clipped to that boundary; edge slivers merge into
    their neighbour so no square is unusably small. Satellite imagery sits underneath,
    which is how the sketch's "road in the middle" turned out to be real, and how the
    neighbour's farm at the southern tip was found and excluded from the plan.
  </p>
  <p>
    All geometry, areas and costs are computed in a small Python model and inlined
    into one HTML file at build time. The page itself has no framework and no build
    step beyond that, so it opens from a chat attachment on any phone.
  </p>

  <div class="phones">
    <figure>
      <img src="/assets/kilya/default-mobile.webp" alt="The planner as it opens: location line, 'Arsa planı' heading with Undo and Edit buttons, and the gridded field on satellite imagery with the existing olives, pecan and fruit trees labelled." loading="lazy" width="780" height="1688" />
      <figcaption>Opens on the owner's actual layout. Existing plantings are labelled "mevcut" (existing).</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src="/assets/kilya/edit-mobile.webp" alt="Edit mode: a selected pecan field outlined in gold with four corner handles, a hint reading 'pull the corner to grow the area', and a strip at the bottom showing the crop, area and cost." loading="lazy" width="780" height="1688" />
      <figcaption>Edit mode. A tapped field shows corner handles and a thin strip: crop, area, cost.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src="/assets/kilya/panel-mobile.webp" alt="The crop panel open over the map: 'What shall we plant here?' with six large colour-swatched buttons for olive, pecan, fruit, alfalfa, sunflower and other." loading="lazy" width="780" height="1688" />
      <figcaption>The crop picker only appears after an explicit "change the crop" tap.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <p>
    Every version was published to a shared link and tried on the owner's own iPhone
    through WhatsApp. Seventy-eight versions shipped in two days, each one answering a
    concrete complaint ("my hand selects an area by accident", "I can't tell the
    cards open", "the scroll stops on the map"). The decisions below are the ones
    that changed the interaction model.
  </p>

  <h2>Key decisions</h2>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>1 — The map is a picture until you press Edit</h3>
    <p>
      Early versions let you drag on the map at any time, which meant a finger that
      wanted to scroll the page got trapped on the parcel. Now the map is inert by
      default: the page scrolls, nothing selects. A single "Düzenle" (Edit) button
      turns the gestures on, scrolls the map to fill the screen, and becomes a gold
      "Bitti" (Done) that is always in reach.
    </p>
    <p class="rejected">
      <strong>Rejected:</strong> blocking page scroll over the parcel with
      <code>touch-action: none</code>. It fixed selection and broke reading.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>2 — Pull a corner, don't paint squares</h3>
    <p>
      The first editing model asked people to tap individual 1-dönüm squares. The
      owner found it fiddly and "not finished". The replacement: any drag that starts
      inside a field grows or shrinks that field in whole-dönüm steps from the
      nearest corner, and adjacent areas with the same crop always merge, even across
      the road. One gesture, no modes.
    </p>
    <p class="rejected">
      <strong>Rejected:</strong> a two-mode picker ("whole area" / "square by
      square"). Removed entirely in version 6.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>3 — Two steps between a tap and a decision</h3>
    <p>
      "My hand picks an area by mistake and a panel full of information jumps up."
      A tap now only highlights the field and shows a thin strip (crop, area, cost).
      The crop buttons, undo and the cost breakdown live behind one primary action,
      "Bitkiyi değiştir" (change the crop). Accidental taps cost nothing.
    </p>
    <p class="rejected">
      <strong>Rejected:</strong> a smaller one-step panel, and long-press to select.
      Neither is discoverable for this user.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>4 — Hints that advance with progress, not with a timer</h3>
    <p>
      There are three things to learn: tap an area, pull a corner, zoom with two
      fingers. Each hint sits in a gold note with its own gesture icon and is
      dismissed by doing the thing, not by waiting. The zoom hint only appears while
      the whole map fits on screen, because that is the only moment it is true.
    </p>
    <p class="rejected">
      <strong>Rejected:</strong> rotating tips on a timer, and a permanent "Tap here"
      bubble on the map. Both were read as noise.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>5 — Colour by the real plant, checked for colour vision</h3>
    <p>
      Crops are coloured like the crop: dark olive green, pecan-shell brown, fresh
      alfalfa green, coral for fruit, sunflower yellow. Every adjacent pair was
      validated at ΔE ≥ 15 (≥ 12 under simulated colour-vision deficiency). The Done
      state is harvest gold rather than green, because a green button on a green map
      disappeared.
    </p>
    <p class="rejected">
      <strong>Rejected:</strong> the design tool's first palette suggestion
      (pink and gold) and a green Done button.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="decision">
    <h3>6 — What already grows there is locked, and free</h3>
    <p>
      The link always opens on the owner's current layout: the existing olives, pecan
      and fruit trees are fixed and cannot be edited. Costs are only counted for new
      dönüm, so the number at the bottom answers the real question: what would the
      change cost, not what the whole farm is worth.
    </p>
    <p class="rejected">
      <strong>Rejected:</strong> a fully editable proposal plan. It kept resetting
      the conversation to "but that part is already planted".
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>The whole gesture, end to end</h3>
  <p>
    Edit, clear a proposed olive block, draw a new field on bare ground, choose
    pecan, pull a corner to grow it. The area and cost update live under the finger.
  </p>

  <div class="phones phones--one">
    <figure>
      <img src="/assets/kilya/resize.gif" alt="Animation: pressing Edit, emptying a field, dragging a new rectangle on empty ground, choosing pecan from the crop panel, then pulling a corner handle so the field grows by whole dönüm squares." loading="lazy" width="390" height="844" />
      <figcaption>Recorded from the demo at phone size.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Try it</h3>
  <p>
    The demo below is the real file, in Turkish because that is the client's
    language. Press <strong>Düzenle</strong>, tap a field, pull a corner.
    <a href="/work/kilya/demo.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open it full-screen</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="demo" id="demo">
    <div class="phone">
      <iframe src="/work/kilya/demo.html" title="Planting planner, live demo (Turkish)" loading="lazy"></iframe>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Outcome &amp; what's next</h2>
  <p>
    The owner uses the plan on their phone and has sent back their own layout, which
    is now the default. Beyond the map, the page carries the olive yield timeline, an
    olive-oil mill section with a machinery list, and a cost breakdown in two
    collapsible cards. The prices are rough 2026 estimates and say so.
  </p>
  <div class="phones phones--two">
    <figure>
      <img src="/assets/kilya/olive-mobile.webp" alt="Olive section: two side-by-side cards comparing normal and dense planting, and a single three-segment timeline bar showing yield in year 2, years 3 to 4 and year 5 onwards." loading="lazy" width="780" height="1688" />
      <figcaption>Yield over time as one bar, light to dark, instead of three rows of numbers.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src="/assets/kilya/costs-mobile.webp" alt="Costs section: a 'Planting' card expanded to list each crop with its area and cost, existing plantings marked with a dash, and a 'Mill machinery' card below." loading="lazy" width="780" height="1688" />
      <figcaption>Costs only for new planting. Existing areas show a dash, not a number.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    What I would do next: an English build of the demo, a "save as image" button that
    was removed late (the owner preferred screenshots), and real quotes from the local
    nursery and irrigation supplier to replace the estimates. A few behaviours (smooth
    scroll on Edit, two-finger zoom) were verified on the owner's iPhone rather than
    in a simulator; the rest of the testing ran in a desktop browser at phone width.
  </p>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  /* Phone screenshots: one, two or three up, scaled to the prose measure */
  .phones {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-block: var(--space-8);
  }
  .phones--two { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
  .phones--one { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 390px); justify-content: center; }
  .phones figure { margin: 0; }
  .phones img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 18px;
    background: var(--surface);
  }
  .phones figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    .phones, .phones--two { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .phones figure { max-width: 390px; margin-inline: auto; }
  }

  /* Live demo in a phone frame; the 390 × 844 page is scaled to the frame width */
  .demo {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-block: var(--space-8);
  }
  .phone {
    position: relative;
    width: min(100%, 390px);
    aspect-ratio: 390 / 844;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 28px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--surface);
    box-shadow: 0 20px 60px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.18);
  }
  .phone iframe {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: 390px;
    height: 844px;
    border: 0;
    transform-origin: top left;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 0.9em;
    padding: 0 0.3em;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: var(--surface);
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Scale the 390 px demo iframe to the phone frame's actual width
  const fit = () => {
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".phone").forEach((ph) => {
      const f = ph.querySelector("iframe");
      if (f) f.style.transform = `scale(${ph.clientWidth / 390})`;
    });
  };
  fit();
  addEventListener("resize", fit);
</script>
